QTranslate
----------

Glossa is an independent successor to QTranslate, the classic Windows
translator. It is not affiliated with or endorsed by QTranslate or its author;
the name is used only to describe compatibility with the classic program and
its files.

Glossa is a clean-room implementation. It contains no code, strings or assets
from the QTranslate executable, which was never decompiled or disassembled.
Its behaviour was studied from the outside: the plain-text files users already
edit (Options.json, the JavaScript service scripts and their helper API,
themes, lang.json) and the running program. The helper API that classic
service scripts call (crates/glossa-modules/src/runtime/js/qtranslate_compat.js)
is written for this project.

Classic service scripts, themes and language files are not part of this
repository or of the release builds. Glossa reads them from the user's own
QTranslate installation when pointed at one.

Third-party code in this repository
-----------------------------------

third_party/eframe
  eframe 0.37.0 from egui, https://github.com/emilk/egui
  MIT OR Apache-2.0, Copyright (c) 2018-2021 Emil Ernerfeldt.
  Vendored with one local fix, marked "Glossa patch" in the source and
  described in third_party/README.md.

chrome-lens-rs (crate chrome_lens_ocr)
  https://github.com/bropines/chrome-lens-rs, MIT, a git dependency of
  crates/glossa-lens: the Google Lens protocol, request and reply. It vendors
  Chromium's third_party/lens_server_proto (BSD-3-Clause, Copyright 2024 The
  Chromium Authors) and grew from KolbyML's chrome-lens-ocr (MIT).

fastframe-scroll
  https://github.com/crmne/fastframe, MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Carmine Paolino.
  The touchpad axis lock in crates/glossa-ui-egui/src/scroll.rs (which axis a
  gesture takes, how long it holds it, and which wheel input is left alone)
  is adapted from its crate fastframe-scroll.

Notable dependencies
--------------------

The release binaries are statically linked with these crates, among others,
each under its own license:

  egui and eframe (MIT OR Apache-2.0), winit (Apache-2.0), glow and wgpu
    (MIT OR Apache-2.0): the window system and rendering. epaint_default_fonts
    embeds the Hack, Ubuntu and Noto Emoji fonts (MIT / Bitstream Vera, Ubuntu
    Font Licence 1.0, SIL OFL 1.1).
  boa (https://boajs.dev, Unlicense OR MIT): the JavaScript engine that runs
    JavaScript modules and classic QTranslate service scripts.
  ureq (MIT OR Apache-2.0) with rustls (Apache-2.0 OR ISC OR MIT) and ring
    (Apache-2.0 AND ISC): HTTP and TLS. webpki-roots carries Mozilla's CA
    certificate list (CDLA-Permissive-2.0).
  tray-icon, muda and global-hotkey from the Tauri project (MIT OR
    Apache-2.0): the tray icon and global hotkeys. arboard (MIT OR
    Apache-2.0): the clipboard. rfd (MIT): file dialogs.
  rodio (MIT OR Apache-2.0) and cpal (Apache-2.0) with Symphonia (MPL-2.0):
    audio playback for text to speech. Symphonia is used unmodified; its
    source is at https://github.com/pdeljanov/Symphonia.
  image, tiny_http and flate2 (MIT OR Apache-2.0), zip (MIT) and libloading
    (ISC); on Linux enigo (MIT), on Windows and macOS xcap (Apache-2.0).
  On Linux, zbus (MIT): D-Bus and the desktop portals; spellbook
    (MPL-2.0): Hunspell dictionaries for spell checking, used unmodified,
    source at https://github.com/helix-editor/spellbook.

The full list, with versions, is in Cargo.lock; each crate's license file
ships with its source on crates.io.

Google Lens
-----------

Glossa talks to Google Lens itself, through chrome-lens-rs (above).
chrome-lens-py (https://github.com/bropines/chrome-lens-py, MIT, by the same
author) is an optional, separate program: when the user has installed it,
Glossa can use its daemon or command line instead. Glossa does not include
it.
